Document (dev.stxt.website): STXT — A Human-First markup language for structured documents
	Metadata:
		Author: Joan Costa Mombiela
		Revision: Claude Fable 5
		Last modif: 2026-09-01
		Description: STXT is a human-first markup language for structured documents and data: no braces, no closing tags, just indentation. Readable by people, reliable for machines.

	Header: @STXT@: Introduction

	Assert >>
		@STXT@ is a **Human-First** markup language, where documents
		are readable and expressive, and at the same time reliable for machines.

	Content >>
		> @STXT@ is pronounced "ESS-text" — first the letter `S`, then `text`, like _S-TEXT_.

	Subheader: Documents

	Content >>
		@STXT@ is a human-readable markup language for structured documents and data: no
		braces, no closing tags, no escape characters — just names, values and indentation.
		It is aimed at:

		* Representing documents and data clearly.
		* Being simple to read and write.
		* Being trivial to parse in any programming language.
		* Allowing both structured content and free text.
		* Applying content rules via `@stxt.schema` or `@stxt.template`.

		An @STXT@ document combines readability with an explicit semantic structure, which
		makes it suitable for [corporate documents](use-cases-corporate-docs),
		[CMS content](use-cases-cms) and [configuration files](use-cases-config-files):

	Code >>
		# A simple email, no validation
		Email:
			From: John Smith
			To: Mery Adams
			Cc: Keyla Brown
			Title: Project report
			# The body is a literal text block
			Body Content >>
				Hello Mery!
				The book is finished!

	Content >>
		Two rules carry most of the language. **Indentation is the structure**: one tab or
		four spaces per level, and the hierarchy on screen is the hierarchy of the document.
		And **free text is literal**: everything indented under a `>>` node is text exactly
		as written, with no escapes and no quotes. The [tutorial](tutorial) covers the rest
		in fifteen short sections.

	Subheader: Documents with namespace and schemas

	Content >>
		@STXT@ allows the use of namespaces to group documents.
		Schemas or templates can also be defined
		to validate the content of a document.

		An `Email` document from the `com.example.docs` namespace, together with the template
		that validates it:

	Code >>
		# The document
		Email (com.example.docs):
			From: John Smith <john.smith@example.com>
			To: Mery Adams <mery.adams@example.com>
			Cc: Keyla Brown <keyla.brown@example.com>
			Title: Project report
			Body Content >>
				Hello Mery!!
				The book is finished!!

		# Its template definition
		Template (@stxt.template): com.example.docs
			Structure >>
				Email (com.example.docs):
					From: EMAIL
					To: EMAIL
					Cc: EMAIL
					Bcc: EMAIL
					Title: (?)
					Body Content: (1) TEXT
					Metadata (org.example.meta): (?)

	Content >>
		Validation is optional and belongs to the language itself: the template above is
		@STXT@ too. A validating tool checks that every node is declared, that cardinalities
		hold and that typed values — dates, numbers, email addresses — are well formed; the
		closed content model rejects a misspelled field instead of accepting it silently.
		How documents, definitions and tools fit together in a project is described in
		[The working environment](working-environment).

	Subheader: How it compares

	Content >>
		@STXT@ shares ground with the usual structured-text formats, and the comparison with
		each one has its own page. [YAML](stxt-vs-yaml) is also indentation-based, but
		optimizes for data serialization rather than for documents; [JSON](stxt-vs-json) is
		a machine format, not meant to be written by hand; [XML](stxt-vs-xml) offers
		validation at the price of verbosity; and [Markdown](stxt-vs-markdown) formats
		prose but does not structure data — the two complement each other. The short
		version of all four, plus TOML, is in the
		[FAQ](faq#why-not-xml-yaml-or-toml), and the reasoning behind the design is in
		[Design principles](design-principles).

	Subheader: Where to start

	Content >>
		* **Read the [tutorial](tutorial)**: a complete document on the first screen, one
		  rule per section after it.
		* **Open the [playground](https://play.stxt.dev)**: editor, validation and examples
		  in the browser, with nothing to install.
		* **Browse the use cases**: [corporate documents](use-cases-corporate-docs),
		  [AI and LLMs](ai-llms), [CMS and publishing](use-cases-cms),
		  [configuration files](use-cases-config-files), [RFCs](use-cases-rfcs) and
		  [contracts](use-cases-contracts).
		* **Install the [tools](tools)**: the `stxt` command line, the VS Code extension and
		  the TypeScript, Java and Python libraries.

		The language is stable: version 1.0 froze the syntax, the canonical tree and the
		error codes. The exact promise is spelled out in
		[Stability and versions](stability), and the [FAQ](faq) answers the rest.
